#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:18
msgid "Get Started with Bluetooth"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:20
msgid "In this project, we demonstrate how to communicate with a Bluetooth module through Arduino."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:22
msgid "Firstly, we need to set up the circuit and use software serial communication. Connect the TX pin of the Bluetooth module to pin 3 of the Uno board, and connect the RX pin of the Bluetooth module to pin 4 of the Uno board."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:25
msgid "1. Build the Circuit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:30
msgid ":ref:`cpn_uno`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:31
msgid ":ref:`cpn_jdy31`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:34
msgid "2. Upload the code"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:36
msgid "Open the ``00-Bluetooth_start.ino`` file under the path of ``ultimate-sensor-kit\\code\\iot_project_esp01\\bluetooth\\00-Bluetooth_start``, or copy this code into **Arduino IDE**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:38
msgid "The code establishes a software serial communication using Arduino’s SoftwareSerial library, allowing the Arduino to communicate with the JDY-31 Bluetooth module through its digital pins 3 and 4 (as Rx and Tx). It checks for data transfer between them, forwarding received messages from one to the other at a baud rate of 9600. **With this code, you can use the Arduino’s serial monitor to send AT commands to the JDY-31 Bluetooth module and receive its responses**."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:46
msgid "3. Configuring the Bluetooth module"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:48
msgid "Click the magnifying glass icon (Serial Monitor) in the upper right corner and set the baud rate to ``9600``. Then, select ``both NL & CR`` from the drop-down option of the ``New Line`` dropdown box."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:52
msgid "The following are some examples of using AT commands to configure Bluetooth modules: Enter ``AT+NAME`` to obtain the name of the Bluetooth device. If you want to modify the Bluetooth name, please add a new name after ``AT+NAME``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:54
msgid "**Query the name of a Bluetooth device:** ``AT+NAME``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:58
msgid "**Set Bluetooth device name:** ``AT+NAME`` (following by the new name). ``+OK`` means the setting was successful. You can send ``AT+NAME`` again to verify."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:63
msgid "To ensure consistency in the learning experience, it is recommended not to modify the default baud rate of the Bluetooth module and **keep it at its default value of 4 (i.e. 9600 baud rate)**. In relevant courses, we communicate with Bluetooth using a baud rate of 9600."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:65
msgid "**Set Bluetooth baudrate:** ``AT+BAUD`` (followed by the number indicating the baudrate)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:67
msgid "4 == 9600"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:68
msgid "5 == 19200"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:69
msgid "6 == 38400"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:70
msgid "7 == 57600"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:71
msgid "8 == 115200"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/iot_project_esp01/00-iot_bluetooth_start.rst:72
msgid "9 == 128000"
msgstr ""

